{"name":"Vitamin C","hex":"#ff9900","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vitamin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a specific group of organic compounds essential in small quantities for healthy human growth, metabolism, development, and body function. They are found in minute amounts in plant and animal foods or sometimes produced synthetically, and deficiencies of specific vitamins produce specific disorders.","preceding a word or its initial letter, to imply that the referent benefits health or wellness"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fortify with vitamins."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vitamin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vitamin C","blocks":[{"text":"Vitamin C or ascorbate is a water-soluble vitamin found in citrus and other fruits, berries and vegetables. It is found as a form of ascorbic acid. It is an essential nutrient involved in the repair of tissue, the formation of collagen, and the enzymatic production of certain neurotransmitters. It is required for the functioning of several enzymes and is important for immune system function. It is also an antioxidant. Most animals are able to synthesize their own vitamin C. However, higher primates, most bats, guinea pigs, some fish species, and some bird species must acquire it from dietary sources because a gene for a synthesis enzyme has mutations that render it dysfunctional; humans obtain this vitamin from those fruits and vegetables."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/L-Ascorbic_acid.svg/330px-L-Ascorbic_acid.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitamin_C"},{"title":"Vitamin C (singer)","blocks":[{"text":"Colleen Ann Fitzpatrick, known professionally as Vitamin C, is an American record executive, singer, songwriter, record producer, and actress. She began her career as an Ivory soap baby and child actress, appearing in John Waters' film Hairspray (1988), and continued to appear in minor roles in films before launching a music career with the alternative rock band Eve's Plum in 1991."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Vitamin_C_2001_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitamin_C_(singer)"},{"title":"Vitamin","blocks":[{"text":"Vitamins are organic molecules that are essential to an organism in small quantities for proper metabolic function. These essential nutrients cannot be synthesized in the organism in sufficient quantities for survival, and therefore must be obtained through consumption. For example, vitamin C can be synthesized by some species but not by others; it is not considered a vitamin in the first instance but is in the second. Most vitamins are not single molecules, but groups of related molecules called vitamers. For example, there are eight vitamers of vitamin E: four tocopherols and four tocotrienols."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/B_vitamin_supplement_tablets.jpg/330px-B_vitamin_supplement_tablets.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitamin"},{"title":"C","blocks":[{"text":"C is the third letter of the Latin alphabet, used in the modern English alphabet, the alphabets of other western European languages and others worldwide. Its name in English is cee, plural cees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Latin_letter_C.svg/330px-Latin_letter_C.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Ascorbic-acid-from-xtal-1997-3D-balls.png/500px-Ascorbic-acid-from-xtal-1997-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Ascorbic-acid-from-xtal-1997-3D-balls.png/500px-Ascorbic-acid-from-xtal-1997-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAscorbic-acid-from-xtal-1997-3D-balls.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vitamin_C_supplements_1.jpg/500px-Vitamin_C_supplements_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vitamin_C_supplements_1.jpg/1280px-Vitamin_C_supplements_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vitamin_C_supplements_1.jpg/500px-Vitamin_C_supplements_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vitamin C supplements","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVitamin_C_supplements_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/VitaminSupplementPills2.jpg/500px-VitaminSupplementPills2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/VitaminSupplementPills2.jpg/1280px-VitaminSupplementPills2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/VitaminSupplementPills2.jpg/500px-VitaminSupplementPills2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vitamin C supplements among other dietary supplements at a US drug store","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVitaminSupplementPills2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Linus_Pauling.jpg/250px-Linus_Pauling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Linus_Pauling.jpg?utm_so